<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Weissenfels</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Weißenfels</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Weissenfels is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. From the late seventeenth century it was the residence of the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-weissenfels">Saxe-Weissenfels</a>, whose court was known for music and opera. The composer Heinrich Schütz grew up there. After the ducal line died out in 1746 it returned to the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/weissenfels.jpg" alt="Weissenfels" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Catatine · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wei%C3%9Fenfels_BlickStadt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
